      "knowledge_point": "Identifying a Radial Line Given Its Polar Equation",
      "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve  $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{10}?$",
      "worked_solution": "The polar curve defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{10}$ is the ray that starts at the origin and makes a counterclockwise angle of $\\frac{11\\pi }{10}$ with the positive $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, the angle it makes with the negative $x$-axis is \n\n$$\n\\frac{11\\pi }{10}-\\pi =\\frac{\\pi }{10}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe correct answer is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/802bb767b28c38598663d928d66338086adf08b80bc5887a7795704183ed2212.png)",
